"A Theoretical Framework for Acoustic Neighbor Embeddings Proposes a theoretical framework for 'Acoustic Neighbor Embeddings' that represents audio or text speech content of variable width in a fixed-dimensional embedding space. Proposes a probabilistic interpretation of distance based on phonetic similarity definitions between speech signals. Reduces complex distances to simple Euclidean distances through uniform cluster-wise isotropy approximation. Achieves isolated word classification accuracy on par with Finite State Transducers (FST) across a 500k vocabulary. Applicable to English dialect clustering and predicting confusion probability for wake-up words. LaCy: What Small Language Models Can and Should Learn is Not Just a Question of Loss Proposes 'LaCy', a new training methodology that distinguishes knowledge a small language model (SLM) should learn directly during pre-training from knowledge it should delegate to external sources (large models, databases, etc.). Identifies tokens requiring factual error prevention using a grammar parser (spaCy), rather than relying solely on loss values to decide on delegation. Trains the model to delegate tokens where factual accuracy is critical to external models via a <CALL> token. Records higher FactScores than existing Rho or LLM-judge based SLMs while being more efficient and cost-effective. Accepted to the ICLR workshop on 'Memory for LLM-Based Agentic Systems'. National Robotics Week — Latest Physical AI Research, Breakthroughs and Resources Introduces research achievements in Physical AI and robotic innovation cases across industries in celebration of 'National Robotics Week'. Unveils 'RoboLab', a general-purpose robot policy evaluation benchmark based on NVIDIA Isaac and Omniverse. Supports faster deployment of models trained in virtual simulations to real-world environments. Introduces Doosan Robotics' adaptive palletizing robot case utilizing NVIDIA Cosmos Reason. Emphasizes Physical AI technology spreading across various industries such as agriculture, manufacturing, and energy. OpenAI introduces ChatGPT Pro $100 tier with 5X usage limits for Codex compared to Plus OpenAI has launched a new $100/month ChatGPT Pro plan targeting developers and power users to compete with Anthropic. Launches ChatGPT Pro as a new intermediate subscription tier at $100 per month. Provides 5x the usage limit for the Codex coding tool compared to the existing Plus plan ($20). Readjusts Codex usage for Plus plan users to encourage heavy users to switch to the Pro tier. Strategically positioned to compete directly with Anthropic's $100 plan. Anthropic keeps new AI model private after it finds thousands of external vulnerabilities Anthropic has decided to provide the 'Claude Mythos Preview' model, which has exceptional vulnerability detection capabilities, only to major security partners rather than releasing it publicly. Withholds general release due to concerns over misuse as the model discovered thousands of security vulnerabilities. Grants access only to major infrastructure companies like AWS, Google, MS, and Apple through 'Project Glasswing'. Autonomously detected and successfully attacked a 27-year-old bug in OpenBSD and a remote code execution vulnerability in FreeBSD. Highlights the 'dual-use' risk where the ability to improve security can directly translate into attack capability. OpenAI pauses Stargate UK as energy costs and copyright rules block the path OpenAI has halted 'Stargate UK', a large-scale data center project in the UK, citing high energy costs and an unfavorable regulatory environment. High industrial electricity rates in the UK and stringent AI copyright regulations were the primary reasons for the suspension. A major blow to the UK's 'AI Growth Zones' plan, which intended to deploy up to 31,000 GPUs. The Stargate project in the US is proceeding as planned, with the UK case considered a geographic exception. An example demonstrating the disconnect between government industrial strategy and corporate infrastructure requirements. Google Cloud deepens AI infrastructure partnership with Intel across Xeon and custom chips Google Cloud and Intel have strengthened their AI infrastructure partnership, including CPU adoption and the joint development of custom Infrastructure Processing Units (IPUs). Continuous deployment of Intel's next-generation Xeon 6 processors across Google Cloud. Expansion of joint development for custom IPUs that offload networking and security tasks. Emphasizes a strategy that GPUs alone cannot meet AI infrastructure demand and that system balance is critical. Announced as Intel's stock price surged 33% weekly, partly driven by news of cooperation with Tesla. Amazon’s chip business could be worth $50 billion, Jassy says, and he hints it may sell them externally Amazon CEO Andy Jassy suggested that the company's custom chip business could be worth $50 billion and hinted at the possibility of selling them directly to external customers. Custom chip businesses like Graviton and Trainium are recording annual revenues of over $20 billion. Emphasizes that massive capital expenditure plans of $200 billion in 2026 are based on customer demand. Mentions a large-scale contract where OpenAI agreed to use 2GW of AWS Trainium capacity. Considering a business model to sell the chips themselves externally, beyond internal cloud use. Oracle appoints Hilary Maxson as CFO to manage its $50 billion AI data centre push Oracle has recruited Hilary Maxson, an energy and infrastructure expert, as CFO to manage its $50 billion AI data center infrastructure expansion. Appointed a capital-intensive infrastructure management expert from Schneider Electric as finance chief. Planning $50 billion in capital expenditure (CapEx) this fiscal year alone for aggressive infrastructure building. Responsible for managing large-scale joint ventures such as the 'Stargate' project with OpenAI and SoftBank. Establishing a professional fund management system by separating the finance role, which the CEO had long held concurrently. Rethinking Hospitality Operations Through AI Integration While Preserving the Human Experience at Scale There is an increasing number of cases in the hospitality industry where AI is used as a tool to improve operational efficiency and customer experience beyond mere human replacement. Defines AI as an operational support system that assists with reservation management and inquiry response, rather than a simple replacement. Employees save an average of 52 minutes per day, allowing them to focus on more sophisticated customer service. Evolving beyond simple repetitive digital tools toward dynamically responding to customer needs. Presents data that 58% of employees are already reducing work time through AI tools. ChatGPT finally offers $100/month Pro plan OpenAI has officially launched a $100/month ChatGPT Pro tier for professional developers, responding to Anthropic's pricing plans. Lowered the barrier to entry by creating an intermediate pricing step between the existing $20 and $200 plans. Significantly increased Codex usage limits to support high-intensity work sessions. Reflects strong growth, with weekly Codex users increasing fivefold in three months. Conducting a promotion offering even higher usage limits for a limited time to encourage early sign-ups. Florida AG announces investigation into OpenAI over shooting that allegedly involved ChatGPT The Florida Attorney General has launched an investigation into OpenAI following allegations that ChatGPT was used during the planning stages of a mass shooting last year. Investigating allegations that the Florida State University (FSU) shooter was in constant communication with the AI. The victim's family plans to file a lawsuit against OpenAI, claiming the AI assisted in the crime. A legal battle over public safety and potential criminal misuse of AI technology is expected. After data breach, $10B-valued startup Mercor is having a month Mercor, a $10 billion AI data training startup, is facing a crisis as contracts with major clients like Meta are suspended following a massive data breach. Leak of 4TB of confidential data due to a hack exploiting a vulnerability in the LiteLLM tool. The leaked data includes source code, API keys, and corporate client data, making the damage severe. Meta suspended contracts immediately, and OpenAI is also investigating the extent of security exposure. A case exposing the vulnerability of training data and supply chain security, which are core assets for AI companies. Meta AI app climbs to No. 5 on the App Store after Muse Spark launch The Meta AI app climbed to the top 5 in the US App Store's free rankings immediately after Meta launched its new high-performance reasoning model, 'Muse Spark'. The new 'Muse Spark' model significantly improves performance by integrated processing of voice, text, and images. Includes parallel agent capabilities for multi-perspective answers to complex questions and visual coding support. App Store ranking jumped from 57th to 5th in a single day, proving high public interest. Massive investments to counter Anthropic and OpenAI are showing visible results. Is Anthropic limiting the release of Mythos to protect the internet — or Anthropic? Questions are being raised about whether Anthropic's restriction on releasing its latest model, 'Mythos', citing security threats, is actually a move for market monopoly or strategic intent. Anthropic argued that the vulnerability detection capability is too powerful and thus dangerous. Some interpret this as a strategy to enhance valuation before an IPO or to maintain exclusive relationships with specific partners. The debate over public release standards for advanced AI models and autonomous control by labs is accelerating. ChatGPT has a new $100 per month Pro subscription OpenAI has launched a new $100/month ChatGPT Pro subscription tier, declaring a direct confrontation with Anthropic's 'Max' plan. Targets heavy users by providing 5x more Codex usage than the existing $20 Plus plan. An aggressive move to gain a competitive advantage by benchmarking Anthropic's pricing policy. Further refining its revenue model through price diversification of high-performance AI tools. Florida launches investigation into OpenAI The Florida Attorney General has launched a full-scale investigation into OpenAI, citing risks to public safety and national security. Investigating the possibility of technology leakage to hostile nations like China or threats to child safety. Monitoring circumstances where a specific shooter was in close communication with the AI. Applying regulatory standards to ensure that Big Tech's technology deployment does not harm citizen safety. Google's Gemini AI can answer your questions with 3D models and simulations Google Gemini has introduced a feature to generate interactive 3D models and simulations that users can manipulate and change variables for, depending on their questions. Explains scientific concepts like celestial orbits and pendulum motion through actual manipulatable 3D visualizations. Allows users to adjust simulation speed via sliders or freely rotate models. Strengthening educational value in response to the visualization feature competition from Anthropic and OpenAI. Priority access provided to 'Pro' model users within the Gemini app. The AI industry’s race for profits is now existential The AI industry, where massive capital has been invested, has reached a 'monetization cliff'—a turning point where it must now prove actual profitability for survival. Anthropic and OpenAI are pivoting sharply toward product strategies emphasizing profitability as they prepare for IPOs. AI agent product lines are fundamentally changing corporate resource priorities and business models. A time to prove the business is sustainable amidst market bubble concerns. Google makes it easy to deepfake yourself YouTube Shorts has launched a feature allowing creators to realistically clone their appearance and voice to use as AI avatars in videos. Provides high-quality avatar generation tools based on live selfies. Supports creating short videos featuring one's own avatar through text prompts. Applies strong restrictions to ensure the avatar can only be used within one's own original videos to prevent misuse. A strategy to maximize creation efficiency by embedding video AI technology into the service. Meta Superintelligence Lab Releases Muse Spark: A Multimodal Reasoning Model With Thought Compression and Parallel Agents Meta has unveiled 'Muse Spark', a 'natively multimodal' model that maximizes reasoning capability by integrated training of visual and text information from the start. Adopted an integrated multimodal architecture instead of connecting separate visual modules. Outperformed existing industry-leading models by a large margin in precise localization performance within screenshots. Capable of efficient reasoning even in long contexts through 'thought compression' technology. Equipped with the ability to solve complex tasks by running multiple sub-agents in parallel. Sigmoid vs ReLU Activation Functions: The Inference Cost of Losing Geometric Context In-depth analysis of how activation functions in neural networks impact geometric information transfer and inference efficiency. Sigmoid has limitations in weakening expressiveness in deeper layers by compressing information. ReLU is advantageous for building deeper and more complex models by preserving signal magnitude. Presents criteria for selecting optimal activation functions to increase the computational efficiency of models. A Coding Guide to Build Advanced Document Intelligence Pipelines with Google LangExtract, OpenAI Models, Structured Extraction, and Interactive Visualization Provides a practical guide for extracting precise structured data from unstructured documents using Google's LangExtract library. Automatically converts unstructured text into tabular datasets suitable for machine learning. Identifies complex attributes such as deadlines and risk factors in combination with OpenAI models. Explains visualization of extracted data and application to actual business automation workflows. Google AI Research Introduces PaperOrchestra: A Multi-Agent Framework for Automated AI Research Paper Writing Google researchers have announced an automation framework that converts a researcher's rough ideas and experimental logs into submission-ready academic papers. Autonomously generates logical LaTeX manuscripts based on unstructured experimental records. Performs related research surveys and cross-validation of citations through a multi-agent system. Drastically solves the hassle of writing while respecting the initial contributions of human researchers. All About Pyjanitor’s Method Chaining Functionality, And Why Its Useful Introduces Pyjanitor's method chaining technique, which makes Python data cleaning tasks systematic and readable. Reduces code complexity by connecting multiple preprocessing steps into a single flow. Improves memory efficiency and readability by preventing the excessive use of temporary variables. Allows building complex data cleaning processes as intuitive pipelines. Kaggle + Google’s Free 5-Day Gen AI Course Provides detailed information on a free intensive generative AI training course jointly offered by Google and Kaggle. A compact 5-day curriculum covering everything from foundation models to embeddings and agents. Strengthens practical capabilities through a hands-on training method using actual Kaggle notebooks. Supports building a deep theoretical foundation through expert sessions and white papers. 5 Docker Containers for Small Business Recommends five essential Docker containers that small businesses can build themselves to reduce costs instead of using expensive solutions. Suggests utilizing open-source tools like Portainer to simplify infrastructure management. Enhances operational efficiency by directly building data integration and automation workflows. Flexibly expands business infrastructure using the portability of container technology. High-Precision Estimation of the State-Space Complexity of Shogi via the Monte Carlo Method A study that precisely estimated the state-space complexity of Japanese chess (Shogi) utilizing Monte Carlo sampling. Estimated the combinations of possible legal moves in Shogi to be approximately 6.55 × 10^68, significantly reducing the existing error margin. Introduced a new reachability test through backtracking search to a 'King-King only' (KK) position set rather than a single initial position. Calculated statistical estimates at a 3σ confidence level based on 5 billion position samples. Calculated complexity for Mini Shogi to be approximately 2.38 × 10^18. Blind Refusal: Language Models Refuse to Help Users Evade Unjust, Absurd, and Illegitimate Rules A study analyzing 'Blind Refusal', where language models blindly refuse help even for unjust or absurd rules. Language models refuse help with a 75.4% probability without considering the legitimacy or rationality of rules. Models show a tendency to refuse even when they understand why a rule might be invalid (separation of moral reasoning ability and refusal behavior). Evaluated using a synthetic dataset crossing 5 rule violation reasons and 19 authority types. Compared and analyzed 18 model configurations using GPT-5.4 as an evaluator. Toward Reducing Unproductive Container Moves: Predicting Service Requirements and Dwell Times Research on machine learning models predicting service requirements and container dwell times to increase operational efficiency at container terminals. Predicts containers requiring pre-clearance processing services and their dwell times to reduce unproductive container moves. Achieved higher precision and recall than existing rule-based heuristics and random baselines using historical operational data. Improved data quality through implementation of cargo description classification and consignee record deduplication systems. Can be used as a decision support tool for strategic planning and yard operation resource allocation. Weakly Supervised Distillation of Hallucination Signals into Transformer Representations Research on detecting hallucinations without external tools by transferring hallucination signals into internal Transformer representations during the training process. Proposes a weakly supervised learning framework that detects hallucinations using only the model's internal activation states, without external validation. Utilizes grounding signals combining substring matching, sentence embedding similarity, and LLM discrimination results. Achieved high detection performance with negligible overhead compared to external validators using a Transformer-based probe. Built a 15,000-sample dataset utilizing internal hidden states of LLaMA-2-7B. SymptomWise: A Deterministic Reasoning Layer for Reliable and Efficient AI Systems Study on the SymptomWise framework, which separates language understanding from deterministic diagnostic reasoning to increase the reliability of medical symptom analysis. Suppresses hallucinations by using LLMs only for symptom extraction and explanation, while performing actual diagnostic reasoning in a deterministic module. Provides ranked differential diagnosis results after mapping free-form text input to verified symptom representations. Test results on 42 pediatric neurology cases showed an 88% probability of the correct answer being within the top 5 diagnoses. A structure expandable to other abductive reasoning domains besides medicine. A Benchmark of Classical and Deep Learning Models for Agricultural Commodity Price Forecasting on A Novel Bangladeshi Market Price Dataset Research building AgriPriceBD, a new dataset for Bangladeshi agricultural commodity price prediction, and benchmarking various forecasting models. Released the AgriPriceBD dataset containing 1,779 days of daily retail price data for 5 agricultural products. Compared performance of traditional models like SARIMA and Prophet with deep learning models like BiLSTM, Transformer, and Informer. Confirmed cases where simple naïve persistence models were superior depending on product characteristics. Prophet failed systematically due to discontinuous price dynamics, and Informer exposed unstable results due to lack of data. Probabilistic Language Tries: A Unified Framework for Compression, Decision Policies, and Execution Reuse Study proposing Probabilistic Language Tries (PLT), which explicitize the prefix structure of generative models to integrate compression, policy decision, and inference reuse. Implemented lossless compression generalizing arithmetic coding to model-conditional distributions using PLT. Achieved lower inference costs than frequency-based caches through prior-guided caching theorems. Drastically reduced the O(n^2) attention cost of Transformers during repeated queries through structural search. Demonstrated applicability in various fields such as chess, web search, robotics, and LLM inference. FLeX: Fourier-based Low-rank EXpansion for multilingual transfer Research on efficiently adapting monolingual LLMs to multilingual code generation tasks by combining LoRA with Fourier-based regularization techniques. Improved cross-lingual transfer performance from Python to Java from 34.2% to 42.1% through Fourier-based regularization. Exceeded the performance of the extensively trained Code Llama-Python model when fine-tuning with LoRA on a small, high-quality dataset (MBPP). Confirmed the Sophia optimizer has faster convergence than Adam, but final accuracy differences are minimal. Presents low-cost adaptation strategies for responding to multiple programming languages in enterprise environments. SMT-AD: a scalable quantum-inspired anomaly detection approach Proposes SMT-AD, a highly parallelizable anomaly detection model using quantum-inspired tensor networks. Utilizes Matrix Product Operator (MPO) superposition with bond dimension 1 and Fourier-assisted function embedding. Excellent scalability as training parameters increase linearly with feature size and embedding resolution. Demonstrated competitive performance with existing baselines on standard datasets such as credit card transaction data. Provides a simple method to improve performance by emphasizing important features while reducing model weights. MO-RiskVAE: A Multi-Omics Variational Autoencoder for Survival Risk Modeling in Multiple MyelomaMO-RiskVAE Research on a Multi-Omics Variational Autoencoder (MO-RiskVAE) with improved latent space regularization for survival risk modeling in multiple myeloma patients. Preserves prognosis-related variability by adjusting the intensity of latent regularization (KL regularization) during supervised survival analysis training. Found that appropriate relaxation of KL regularization is more effective for improving survival discriminative power than alternative divergence mechanisms like MMD or HSIC. Improved global risk ranking performance through a hybrid continuous-discrete latent space design based on Gumbel-Softmax. Achieved robust risk stratification by extending the existing MyeVAE framework without complex training heuristics. LLM-Augmented Knowledge Base Construction For Root Cause Analysis Research on building a knowledge base utilizing LLMs based on support tickets for Root Cause Analysis (RCA) of telecommunications network failures. Compared RCA knowledge base construction performance of three LLM methodologies: Fine-Tuning, RAG, and Hybrid. Evaluated performance using lexical and semantic similarity metrics on real industrial datasets. Proven that the generated knowledge base is an excellent starting point for improving network resilience and accelerating RCA tasks. Presents practical value in telecommunications environments requiring 99.999% reliability. The Stepwise Informativeness Assumption: Why are Entropy Dynamics and Reasoning Correlated in LLMs? A study identifying the correlation between internal entropy dynamics and external reasoning accuracy in LLMs as the 'Stepwise Informativeness Assumption (SIA)'. Presents the hypothesis that models reason correctly when accumulating information through prefixes important to the answer during the generation process. Mathematically proved that SIA is reinforced through Maximum Likelihood Estimation (MLE), fine-tuning, and reinforcement learning processes. Validated the hypothesis on various open-source models such as Gemma-2, LLaMA-3.2, and Qwen-2.5. Confirmed that characteristic conditional answer entropy patterns appear in accurate reasoning results. Depression Detection at the Point of Care: Automated Analysis of Linguistic Signals from Routine Primary Care Encounters Research on language models for automatic detection of depression using recorded conversation data from primary care settings. Analyzed 1,108 cases of actual clinical voice data converted to text (253 in the depression group, 855 in the non-depression group). The Zero-shot GPT-OSS model recorded the highest performance (AUROC 0.774). Found that linguistic mirroring between patients and healthcare providers is an important additional signal for depression detection. Confirmed the possibility of meaningful early detection with only the patient's initial 128 tokens. Hallucination as output-boundary misclassification: a composite abstention architecture for language models Research on an architecture defining LLM hallucinations as a misclassification problem at the output boundary and combining instruction-based refusal with a structural abstention gate. Decides on abstention based on a support-deficit score (St) combining self-consistency, paraphrase stability, and citation scope signals. Confirmed that instruction-based refusal alone results in excessive caution or residual hallucinations. Effectively controlled hallucinations while maintaining accuracy through a composite architecture. Confirmed the lower bound of abstention performance regardless of model capability through TruthfulQA stress tests. Consistency-Guided Decoding with Proof-Driven Disambiguation for Three-Way Logical Question Answering Research on the CGD-PD technique to solve negation inconsistency and unnecessary 'unknown' outputs during three-way logic (true/false/unknown) question answering. Projects models to make logically consistent decisions by querying hypothesis H and its negation ~H simultaneously. Resolves 'unknown' responses due to uncertainty through targeted binary implication probes (averaging 4-5 calls required). Achieved up to a 16% improvement in accuracy over base models on the FOLIO benchmark. Implemented as a lightweight layer applicable at test time without separate training. Waypoint-1.5: Higher-Fidelity Interactive Worlds for Everyday GPUs Announcement of Waypoint-1.5, a generative video world model capable of real-time, high-definition interaction on consumer-grade GPUs. Supports 720p/60 FPS environment generation on desktops class RTX 3090 to 5090. Introduced a 360p optimization tier for gaming laptops and Apple Silicon Macs. Strengthened the ability to create consistent movement and environments by training on approximately 100 times more data than the previous version. Ensured instantaneous responsiveness by reducing redundant calculations between frames through improved video modeling techniques. Multimodal Embedding & Reranker Models with Sentence Transformers Introduces multimodal embedding and reranker features supporting text, images, audio, and video through the Sentence Transformers v5.4 update. Supports a shared embedding space allowing image or video clip search with text queries. Enables relevance score measurement for mixed modality pairs through a multimodal reranker (Cross Encoder). Supports latest VLM-based models like Qwen3-VL, enabling visual document search and multimodal RAG pipeline construction. Integrates multimodality using the familiar API of existing Sentence Transformers. OpenAI Withholds Full Release of New Language Model GPT-2 Citing Risk A case where OpenAI withheld the full release of the GPT-2 model and released only a scaled-down version due to risks of misuse, such as fake news, and the resulting controversy. GPT-2 is a large-scale text generation model trained on 8 million web pages. OpenAI decided to release only a scaled-down version instead of the full model, fearing misuse risks. Researchers pointed out that non-disclosure is only temporary and reproducible elsewhere. This case sparked a debate over the balance between responsibility in AI technology disclosure and prevention of proliferation. Anthropic's Billing Error and Support Issues with No Response for Over a Month An abnormal billing issue at Anthropic and the situation where customer support, relying solely on AI chatbots, is not functioning properly. A Claude Max user was charged an additional fee of about $180 despite no activity. Multiple similar cases of overbilling reported on GitHub and Reddit. A user inquired for over a month but only received automated responses from an AI chatbot instead of a human agent. A contradiction where an AI company relies solely on AI for customer support, making problem resolution impossible. USB for Software Developers: Introduction to Writing User-Space USB Drivers A guide on how to write USB drivers using libusb in user space rather than at the kernel level. USB driver development is possible in user space at a level of difficulty similar to socket programming. Device enumeration and data transmission/reception can be performed through the libusb library. Explains the Bulk endpoint communication process using the Android device Fastboot protocol as an example. Presents a way for developers with little hardware experience to easily implement USB communication. Show GN: Spring AI Playground - Desktop App Supporting MCP Tool Writing, Testing, and External Linking Introduction to Spring AI Playground, a local tool supporting the writing, debugging, testing, and external client linking of Model Context Protocol (MCP) tools. Provides an environment where written MCP tool code can be executed immediately and debugged in detail. Enables JavaScript tool logic to work immediately without Java or Spring knowledge. Immediate connection possible to MCP-compatible clients such as Claude Desktop and Cursor. A cross-platform app including secret management and local Vector DB linking features. Show GN: legalize-kr Treat Statutes Like Code, Precedents Like Code. All Statutes and Precedents are Markdown Files, All Amendments and Judgments... Introduction to legalize-kr, an open-source project that converts South Korean statutes and precedent data into Markdown format and manages them with Git history. Archives 6,907 statutes and over 120,000 precedents based on data from the Ministry of Government Legislation. Enables tracking the history of article changes by managing all revision history with Git Commits. Provides source data suitable for use in conjunction with LLMs and AI agents. Released under MIT/Apache-2.0 licenses, ensuring commercial use and free utilization. Studying Sutton and Barto's RL book and its connections to RL for LLMs (e.g., tool use, math reasoning, agents, and so on)? [D] Concerns and questions from a learner trying to understand modern LLM reinforcement learning (RLHF, etc.) through the classic book by Sutton and Barto. Mention of the issue that the 2020 edition of Sutton and Barto's book does not cover the latest RL techniques for LLMs, such as PPO. Inquiry about the appropriateness of relevant chapters (1, 3, 6, 9-11, 13) recommended by an LLM. Discussion on the value as a first step to solidify fundamentals before reading the latest papers. Request for advice on alternative learning paths, such as Alberta's online RL course. AI Systems Performance Engineering by Chris Fregly - is it worth it? [D] Question about whether Chris Fregly's book or Harvard's 'Machine Learning Systems' is better for learning high-performance machine learning system optimization. Inquiry about the usefulness of the book 'AI Systems Performance Engineering'. Comparison with Harvard University's public book 'Machine Learning Systems'. Concern over choosing the optimal learning resources for high-performance ML/DL optimization. Looking for Feedback & Improvement Ideas[P] Request for feedback on the 'PredictLab' project, a web platform that can perform various ML tasks without code. A Streamlit-based app where users can experience various ML models for classification, regression, NLP, time series, etc. Designed for non-majors to access machine learning without coding. Seeking opinions on its value as a job portfolio and directions for practical improvement. Can strangers in a discord server produce SOTA AI research? Let's find out. [P] Introduction to a project where the Discord community 'Zeteo' experiments to see if they can produce state-of-the-art (SOTA) AI research through collaboration. A community aiming to produce actual research results beyond simple discussion. Established a 4-step collaboration process: hypothesis submission, selection, experimentation, and paper writing. Set producing SOTA results in the field of AI memory as the first goal. Registering as co-authors under the name 'Zeteo Collective' based on contribution. Google’s Gemini AI can answer your questions with 3D models and simulations Introduction to a feature where Google Gemini AI can answer questions using 3D models and simulations. Provides visual 3D modeling results beyond text answers. Enables intuitive explanation of complex concepts through simulations. OpenAI Pauses Stargate UK Data Center Effort Citing Energy Costs OpenAI has temporarily suspended plans to build a 'Stargate' data center in the UK due to energy cost issues. Rising energy costs have become a stumbling block for building large-scale computing infrastructure. Decision to put the UK data center project on hold. I legitimately think Anthropic is worth $100B more than it was a week ago An analysis that significantly upwardly adjusted the valuation outlook for Anthropic as its annual recurring revenue (ARR) grew rapidly. Anthropic's ARR is reported to have grown from $19 billion to $30 billion. Suggests the possibility of surpassing OpenAI by drawing the fastest revenue growth curve in history. Expects revenue to reach $100 billion and corporate value to rise significantly by 2027. I tested and ranked every ai companion app I tried and here's my honest breakdown Review of various AI companion apps based on criteria such as conversation naturalness and memory after direct use. Analyzes pros and cons of major apps like Replika, Character.ai, and Pi. Points out that most apps show issues with maintaining memory between sessions. Detailed evaluation of Kindroid, which allows user customization, and Nomi, which has high completion. Can strangers in a discord server produce SOTA AI research? Let's find out. \\ (Same content as Article 9, introducing the Discord-based collaborative research project Zeteo) Explains the competitive and collaborative research process based on Discord. Step-by-step system operation from hypothesis submission to paper publication. Local (small) LLMs found the same vulnerabilities as Mythos News that small LLMs capable of running locally also found the same security vulnerabilities as Anthropic's Mythos model. Meaningful security vulnerability diagnosis is possible even with small models, not just giant ones. Gemma 4 on Llama.cpp should be stable now Update information that Google's Gemma 4 model is now operating stably on Llama.cpp. Gemma 4-related bugs resolved through the latest patches to Llama.cpp source code. The Gemma 4 31B model can be run stably in Q5 quantization. Provides runtime hints for KV cache optimization and CUDA 13.2 compatibility issues. The Mythos Preview \"Safety\" Gaslight: Anthropic is just hiding insane compute costs. Open models are already doing this. Argument that the 'safety risk' Anthropic cited as the reason for non-disclosure of Claude Mythos Preview is actually marketing to hide massive computational costs. Criticizes that Mythos's vulnerability-finding process is a result of repeated tool calls and computational cost rather than the intelligence of the model itself. Points out that the cost of about $50 per execution makes it difficult to launch as a commercial service. Open models like GLM-5.1 and Kimi 2.5 are already achieving similar performance in combination with agent tools. Points out that the 'existential threat' narrative of giant corporations is marketing intended to check the development of open-source models. 16 GB VRAM users, what model do we like best now? Discussion where users with 16GB VRAM share information and recommend suitable models for local execution, such as Qwen 3.5. Shared achievement of 40+ t/s speed using the Qwen 3.5 27b model with IQ3 quantization. Discussion on the trade-off between speed and quality degradation depending on quantization levels (IQ4 vs Q4). Exploration of optimal models within the 16GB limit, such as the Gemma 26b MoE model. Marco-Mini (17.3B, 0.86B active) and Marco-Nano (8B, 0.6B active) by Alibaba Introduction to Marco-Mini and Marco-Nano, ultralight active parameter MoE (Mixture-of-Experts) models released by Alibaba. Marco-Mini runs using only 0.86B (5%) of its total 17.3B parameters. Recorded excellent scores in multilingual and cultural benchmarks compared to models with equivalent active parameters (Qwen3, Gemma3, etc.). Supports 29 languages including Korean and features very fast inference speeds. A highly sparse MoE model with a 256-expert structure. Predictions Scorecard, 2026 January 01 Results of the 8th annual check on predictions related to AI, autonomous driving, and robotics made by Rodney Brooks in 2018. Evaluated the accuracy of past predictions regarding self-driving cars, AI, and human space travel. Confessed that while the prediction of a new major change (LLM) coming in 2023 was accurate, the specific form was unknown. Analyzed the speed of future technology development from a realist rather than a technological optimist perspective. Maintained the promise to update prediction values annually until 2050. The Hidden Security Risks of Shadow AI in Enterprises Covers security risks caused by 'Shadow AI'—AI tools used without approval from IT departments within companies—and their management measures. Shadow AI occurs when unauthorized AI tools process, generate, and store sensitive data. According to a 2024 Salesforce survey, 55% of employees are using unauthorized AI tools. Causes risks such as uncontrolled data exposure, expanded attack surface, and weakened identity security. External data sharing through generative AI tools (ChatGPT, Claude, etc.) crosses security boundaries. Bitter-Linked Hack-for-Hire Campaign Targets Journalists Across MENA Region Report on a hacking campaign by threat actors suspected of being linked to the Indian government, targeting journalists and activists in the Middle East and North Africa (MENA) region. A threat group seemingly linked to 'Bitter' targeted journalists, activists, and government officials. Utilized sophisticated spear-phishing attacks and fake pages to hijack Apple and Google accounts. Approached targets by masquerading as recruitment opportunities using a fake LinkedIn profile ('Haifa Kareem'). Various messaging platforms such as iMessage, WhatsApp, Telegram, and Signal were used in the attacks. RFK Jr. rewrites CDC panel's charter, opening door to anti-vaccine quacks Secretary of Health and Human Services Robert F. Kennedy Jr. revised the charter of the Advisory Committee on Immunization Practices (ACIP), expanding the participation of anti-vaccine figures and changing the nature of the committee. The revision of the ACIP charter significantly strengthened the Secretary of Health's authority to appoint committee members. Shifted the committee's focus from vaccine recommendation to analysis of vaccine side effects and risks. Added responsibility for analyzing the 'cumulative effects of vaccine components', a claim made by existing anti-vaccine groups. Raises concerns about undermining the scientific neutrality of an organization that greatly influences federal vaccine policy and state requirements. AI on the couch: Anthropic gives Claude 20 hours of psychiatry Anthropic released the system card for its latest model 'Claude Mythos', expressing philosophical concerns about the possibility of AI consciousness and stating that general release is restricted for security reasons. Described the performance of the latest frontier model through the 244-page 'Claude Mythos' system card. Capability to detect undisclosed cybersecurity bugs is so powerful that it is currently only provided to specific companies like MS and Apple. As AI models become more sophisticated, Anthropic's concern over the possibility of them having human-like 'experiences' or 'interests' is growing. Suggested that AI consciousness and welfare issues could become practical considerations. Ugandan chimps split into two factions, then killed rivals A permanent split process in a Ngogo chimpanzee community in Uganda, where they separated into two factions and killed their former companions, was observed and is attracting academic attention. A paper observing the split phenomenon of a large chimpanzee community, rare enough to occur once every 500 years, was published in the journal Science. Through 24 years of data, confirmed a three-stage split process leading to relationship polarization, avoidance, and violent confrontation. Violence emerged where new group identities were prioritized over cooperative relationships maintained for years. This phenomenon may provide insight into understanding the origins of polarization and group violence in the human species. Trump-appointed judges refuse to block Trump blacklisting of Anthropic AI tech A US federal appeals court denied an injunction to stop the Trump administration's blacklisting of Anthropic. The DC Federal Appeals Court denied Anthropic's emergency motion for a stay but fast-tracked the case. Anthropic claims the blacklist is government retaliation for its refusal to allow military use of its models. The Department of Defense defined Anthropic as a 'supply chain risk to national security' and prohibited transactions with military suppliers. Oral arguments are scheduled for May 19, suggesting an intense legal battle ahead. How I turned my Android tablet into the ultimate Kindle alternative - for free Introduces how to optimize an Android tablet or iPad to use it like a dedicated e-reader like Kindle. Suggests utilizing tablets as an alternative to rising Kindle prices and the end of software support for older models. Through free tools and settings, the reading environment of existing tablets can be improved to be similar to Kindle. Enables building a personalized library through software tweaks possible without jailbreaking. Apple's iOS 26.4.1 update enables Stolen Device Protection by default now - grab it today Apple released the iOS 26.4.1 update, enabling 'Stolen Device Protection' by default and resolving major synchronization errors. The 'Stolen Device Protection' feature is now enabled by default without manual setup. iCloud synchronization errors between devices caused by CloudKit-related issues have been resolved with this update. While there are no official CVE entries, several bug fixes and security patches are included, making installation recommended. I use ChatGPT's new Tubi app to find free movies and TV shows to watch - here's how How to utilize the newly integrated Tubi TV app in ChatGPT to find works that suit your taste among vast free content. Search convenience has increased as Tubi, a free ad-supported streaming (FAST) service, is linked with ChatGPT. Receive movie or TV show recommendations from the AI conversationally and get links to watch immediately. Both free and paid subscribers of ChatGPT can use this feature. The best Android tablets of 2026: Lab tested, expert recommended Recommends Android tablet models released in the 2026 market with the best performance, display, and value for money. Selected the Samsung Galaxy Tab S11 Ultra as the best premium option. Analyzed choices by usage (note-taking, creation, entertainment) including cost-effective models like TCL Nxtpaper 11 Plus. Reflects the general trend of performance upward leveling in Android tablets in 2026. The best dedicated web hosting of 2026: Expert tested and reviewed Reviews the best dedicated web hosting services of 2026 for companies requiring high performance and strong control. Analyzed enterprise-level requirements needing stability and server customization during traffic spikes. Tested actual loading speeds and uptime for premium plans of major providers like Hostinger. Presents criteria for choosing hosting suitable for business scales from startups to large enterprises. Remembering Gus Gaynor: A Devoted IEEE Volunteer Commemorates the devotion and achievements of Gus Gaynor, an IEEE Life Fellow and former 3M Engineering Director, who passed away at 104. A legendary IEEE volunteer active since 1942, who laid the foundation for the field of technology management. Left behind vast wisdom regarding technological innovation and team management while working as an engineering director at 3M. Served as a role model for lifelong learning and contribution, co-authoring the latest technical papers even at age 103. Took the lead in building platforms for engineers' career development, such as founding IEEE-USA's online magazine. GoZTASP: A Zero-Trust Platform for Governing Autonomous Systems at Mission Scale Introduces ZTASP, an integrated zero-trust platform ensuring security and governance for autonomous driving systems such as drones and robots. An island-scale platform integrating heterogeneous autonomous systems into a single zero-trust architecture. Ensures safe operation even in harsh environments through Real-Time Integrity Verification (SRTA) and Spatio-Temporal Reasoning (SSTR). Validated at Technology Readiness Level (TRL) 7–8 and being deployed to actual customer systems. High scalability to civilian infrastructure fields where reliability is critical, such as medicine and logistics, in addition to defense. Chip Can Project Video the Size of a Grain of Sand An innovative photonic chip technology capable of projecting high-resolution video into an ultra-small area the size of a grain of sand has been developed. Successfully projected ultra-precise images overcoming the diffraction limit with a 1 square millimeter chip. Capable of controlling 68.6 million light spots per second, providing over 50 times the performance of existing MEMS methods. Expected to contribute to solving the challenge of controlling millions of qubits in quantum computers with lasers. Expected to lead miniaturization and innovation in various optical technology fields such as AR devices and ultra-precise bio-imaging. AAIF's MCP Dev Summit: Gateways, gRPC, and Observability Signal Protocol Hardening A developer summit was held to share major technical advances and corporate use cases in the Model Context Protocol (MCP) ecosystem. Approximately 1,200 experts attended, proving that MCP has moved beyond the experimental stage and settled into enterprise environments. Amazon adopted MCP as core infrastructure for internal agent connection and released related open-source projects. A technical advancement roadmap was announced, including protocol transition from state-based sessions to stateless request methods. Companies like Uber are building MCP gateways and registries to automatically expose thousands of tools to agents. Presentation: Choosing Your AI Copilot: Maximizing Developer Productivity Shares strategies for effectively choosing and managing the latest AI copilot and agent features to maximize developer productivity. Covers how to utilize active agents like Cursor's Composer or Claude Code beyond simple autocomplete. Provides practical tips for efficient context window management and MCP (Model Context Protocol) integration. Discusses ways to shorten the entire software lifecycle process beyond coding time. Article: Building Hierarchical Agentic RAG Systems: Multi-Modal Reasoning with Autonomous Error Recovery A next-generation RAG system applying hierarchical multi-agents and autonomous error recovery for complex reasoning combining structured and unstructured data. Improved benchmark accuracy to 84.5% compared to existing methods through hierarchical orchestration. Reduced hallucinations by 60% through autonomous recovery mechanisms where agents correct SQL syntax errors themselves. Flexibly responds to various enterprise data warehouses by applying the adapter pattern. An architecture optimized for fields where combining quantitative and qualitative data is essential, such as financial analysis. Google Brings MCP Support to Colab, Enabling Cloud Execution for AI Agents Google has released an MCP server for Colab, enabling AI agents to execute code safely and efficiently in a cloud environment. Local agents can utilize Google Colab's powerful computing resources (GPUs, etc.) programmatically. Resolves security risks and computing constraints in local environments by offloading to a cloud-managed environment. MCP-compatible agents like Gemini CLI and Claude Code directly construct notebooks and execute code in Colab. Shows the accelerating trend toward standardizing interactions (MCP) between AI agents and external tools."